  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 216 PM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nRest of This Afternoon and Tonight...\n\nWeak lift well ahead of the surface cold front produced isolated \nshowers across the northern forecast area earlier. These have since \nexited, but the lift continues to produce some clouds across \nthe area. Can't rule out another isolated shower developing through \nmid-afternoon with this lift.\n\nA line of showers and storms has developed in central Illinois this \nafternoon. This line is ahead of the surface cold front, which is \nfarther to the northwest in Illinois. Instability continues to \nincrease ahead of this line, with MLCAPE over 1500 J/kg. With some \nheating and moisture advection to go before the line arrives in \ncentral Indiana, the storms should become more numerous and \nintensify. \n\nThe line will move through the area from northwest to southeast late \nthis afternoon through this evening. Decent shear will work with the \naforementioned instability to keep a threat of some severe storms \naround. The primary threats will be damaging winds and large hail. \nHowever, isolated tornadoes cannot be ruled out, especially north \nwhere shear is best.\n\nAfter midnight, only the southeast third or so will still see some \nscattered convection around. Instability will have waned by then, \nending the severe threat. By 3 or 4 AM, convection should have \nexited the area.\n\nDrier air will lag well behind the front, and winds will diminish \novernight. This will set up the potential for some fog, especially \nsouth where rain will have fallen last. \n\nLows will generally be in the 60s.\n\nThursday...\n\nThe upper trough will linger across the area on Thursday. Low level \nmoisture will remain. Any fog will lift early in the day, with a \nscattered to broken cumulus field developing. Some CAMs generate \nisolated convection Thursday afternoon with some weak forcing from \nthe upper trough. This certainly cannot be ruled out, but for now \nfeel odds are below mentioning in the forecast at this time.\n\nHighs will be in the lower and middle 80s.\n\nFriday through Monday...\n\nAn upper high will nudge its way into the area during this period. \nThis will keep the area dry with increasingly warmer temperatures.\n\nInitially dewpoints will be relatively low, but they will increase \nSunday into Monday, making conditions more humid to go along with \nthe elevated temperatures. \n\nHighs in the lower to middle 80s on Friday will warm to the middle \n80s to lower 90s by Sunday. On Monday, highs could reach the upper \n80s to middle 90s. Heat indices may be near 100 degrees on Monday.\n\nTuesday and beyond...\n\nUncertainty increases in how much influence the upper high will \ncontinue to have across the area. For now, believe it will hang on, \nso will go dry the vast majority of the area on Tuesday. By \nWednesday, it may lose enough influence for isolated convection to \ndevelop in the afternoon. \n\nHeat will continue, with highs most likely from near 90 to the \nmiddle 90s. Heat indices near 100 would also then be likely.",
